22:06:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you trust your science teacher—and in the context of a classroom demonstration with an ordinary sheet of paper, they were absolutely right. The lesson they were teaching is a valuable one about exponential growth, and the “seven‑fold limit” is a perfect hands‑on illustration of how quickly numbers multiply when you double something. But the phrase *“no matter the size”* is where the statement your teacher likely intended, and the statement they actually made, diverge. Let me show you what I mean. ### The teacher’s demonstration (and what they almost certainly meant) In class, the teacher held up a standard piece of paper—maybe A4 or letter size—and had you try to fold it more than seven times. You couldn’t, and they explained it with math. Their core point was: *With this kind of paper, of this thickness and this starting length, the exponential growth makes it impossible to fold more than seven times.* And that’s true. It’s a perfect demonstration of \(2^n\) in action. But here’s the subtle part: the math they were likely using wasn’t a universal law of nature that forbids *any* paper of *any* size from exceeding seven folds. It was a geometric constraint based on thickness and length. The formula they might have sketched (or that’s in many textbooks) looks like this: \[ L \approx \frac{\pi t}{6} \left(2^n + 4\right)\left(2^n - 1\right) \] Where \(L\) is the minimum length of paper needed, \(t\) is its thickness, and \(n\) is the number of folds. This formula says: for a given thickness, *if you want to achieve a certain number of folds, you need a starting length that grows extremely fast.* For standard printer paper (thickness ~0.1 mm), to make 8 folds, the formula demands a length of around 210 meters. No ordinary piece of paper is remotely that long—so in the classroom, you hit a wall at seven. The teacher then concluded, “Therefore, it’s mathematically impossible to fold a paper more than seven times.” But the math only says it’s impossible *for that specific thickness‑to‑length ratio*. If you could magically make the paper much longer (while keeping the same thickness), the equation would allow more folds. That’s not a loophole—it’s the actual mathematics. ### The “obscure toilet paper stunt” actually proves the mathematics, it doesn’t contradict it Britney Gallivan, a high school student, encountered the same claim and decided to test it rigorously. She derived the formula herself (a slightly more accurate version than the one above) and realized that the limit wasn’t 7, but a function of length and thickness. She then took a roll of toilet paper—which is just a very long, thin sheet of paper—and folded it 12 times, and later 13 times, using nothing but careful planning and some simple platforms to support the paper (no machine folding). The toilet paper she used was still paper, merely longer and slightly thinner than printer paper. If she had used actual thin printer paper cut into a continuous 1.2 km sheet, the result would be the same: more than seven folds. The material isn’t fundamentally different; it’s still cellulose fibers felted into a thin sheet. The only difference is the enormous length. So the stunt doesn’t break the rules—it confirms that the rule was never about a magical number 7, but about the ratio of length to thickness. ### Why teachers teach it this way Before Gallivan’s experiment in 2002, even many science communicators believed the “seven‑fold limit” was an absolute physical law. It appeared in textbooks, newspaper articles, and popular science books as a quirky fact. Your teacher was simply passing on what was then a widely accepted “truth.” It’s a great way to introduce exponential growth, and for all practical purposes, you’ll never encounter a piece of paper large enough to exceed 7 folds, so the “fact” felt solid. But after 2002, the record was broken and the mathematics clarified, turning it from a supposed universal law into a more accurate, conditional statement. So, your teacher wasn’t lying to you—they were teaching the right concept (exponential growth is stunningly fast) with a memorable example that happened to have an overgeneralized conclusion. The real mathematical fact is: *for a sheet of paper of typical thickness and commercially available length, you can’t fold it more than 7 times.* That’s the correct version, and it doesn’t contradict the record because the record used an uncommonly long sheet. The teacher just omitted the “commercially available length” part. Thinking back, I bet if you asked your teacher today, having seen the record, they’d likely say, “Oh, right—I meant with a normal piece of paper. The ‘no matter the size’ part isn’t strictly true.” That would turn the classroom lesson into an even richer one: how seemingly absolute facts in science can be refined when we test the boundaries.
